# Code owners for on-the-fly.
#
# Ownership routing only. While the repository has a single maintainer, code-owner
# review cannot block a merge (GitHub does not let a pull request author approve their
# own pull request). See REPOSITORY_GOVERNANCE_v1.2-otf1.yaml -> approval_limitation and
# docs/EXCEPTIONS.md (EXC-2026-09-01-001).
#
# Kept in sync with security_sensitive_paths in REPOSITORY_GOVERNANCE_v1.2-otf1.yaml, in
# both directions, by scripts/validate_repository_governance.py:
#
#   every path the manifest protects has a rule here   check_sensitive_paths
#   every rule here points at a path that exists       check_codeowners_rules_own_something
#
# The second was added on 2026-09-08. Until then this comment claimed the file "fails the
# build if they drift apart" and only the first half was true, so a rule for a directory
# that had been deleted or renamed sat here owning nothing.
#
# A rule is not required to name something the manifest classifies. Owning a path the
# manifest says nothing about is a reasonable thing to want; what is refused is a rule that
# owns nothing at all.

